## an5.30
an5.30:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: Thus have I heard.
an5.30:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was wandering in the land of the Kosalans together with a large Saṅgha of mendicants when he arrived at a village of the Kosalan brahmins named Icchānaṅgala. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was wandering on tour among the Kosalans together with a large Saṅgha of bhikkhus when he reached the Kosalan brahmin village named Icchānaṅgala.
an5.30:1.3 | S: He stayed in a forest near Icchānaṅgala. | T: There the Blessed One dwelled in the Icchānaṅgala woodland thicket.
an5.30:1.4 | S: The brahmins and householders of Icchānaṅgala heard: | T: The brahmin householders of Icchānaṅgala heard:
an5.30:1.5 | S: “It seems the ascetic Gotama—a Sakyan, gone forth from a Sakyan family—has arrived at Icchānaṅgala. He is staying in a forest near Icchānaṅgala. | T: “It is said that the ascetic Gotama, the son of the Sakyans who went forth from a Sakyan family, has arrived at Icchānaṅgala and is now dwelling in the Icchānaṅgala woodland thicket.
an5.30:1.6 | S: He has this good reputation: | T: Now a good report about that Master Gotama has circulated thus:
an5.30:1.7 | S: ‘That Blessed One is perfected, a fully awakened Buddha, accomplished in knowledge and conduct, holy, knower of the world, supreme guide for those fit for training, teacher of gods and humans, awakened, blessed.’ | T: ‘That Blessed One is an arahant, perfectly enlightened, accomplished in true knowledge and conduct, fortunate, knower of the world, unsurpassed leader of persons to be tamed, teacher of devas and humans, the Enlightened One, the Blessed One.
an5.30:1.8 | S: He has realized with his own insight this world—with its gods, Māras, and divinities, this population with its ascetics and brahmins, gods and humans—and he makes it known to others. | T: Having realized by his own direct knowledge this world with its devas, Māra, and Brahmā, this population with its ascetics and brahmins, its devas and humans, he makes it known to others.
an5.30:1.9 | S: He teaches Dhamma that’s good in the beginning, good in the middle, and good in the end, meaningful and well-phrased. And he reveals a spiritual practice that’s entirely full and pure. | T: He teaches a Dhamma that is good in the beginning, good in the middle, and good in the end, with the right meaning and phrasing; he reveals a spiritual life that is perfectly complete and pure.’
an5.30:1.10 | S: It’s good to see such perfected ones.” | T: Now it is good to see such arahants.”
an5.30:1.11 | S: Then, when the night had passed, they took abundant fresh and cooked foods and went to the forest near Icchānaṅgala, where they stood outside the gates making a colossal racket. | T: Then, when the night had passed, the brahmin householders of Icchānaṅgala took abundant food of various kinds and went to the Icchānaṅgala woodland thicket. They stood outside the entrance making an uproar and a racket.
an5.30:2.1 | S: Now, at that time Venerable Nāgita was the Buddha’s attendant. | T: Now on that occasion the Venerable Nāgita was the Blessed One’s attendant.
an5.30:2.2 | S: Then the Buddha said to Nāgita, | T: The Blessed One addressed the Venerable Nāgita:
an5.30:2.3 | S: “Nāgita, who’s making that colossal racket? You’d think it was fishermen hauling in a catch!” | T: “Who is making such an uproar and a racket, Nāgita? One would think it was fishermen at a haul of fish.”
an5.30:2.4 | S: “Sir, it’s these brahmins and householders of Icchānaṅgala. They’ve brought many fresh and cooked foods, and they’re standing outside the gates wanting to offer it specially to the Buddha and the mendicant Saṅgha.” | T: “Bhante, these are the brahmin householders of Icchānaṅgala who have brought abundant food of various kinds. They are standing outside the entrance, wishing to offer it to the Blessed One and the Saṅgha of bhikkhus.”
an5.30:2.5 | S: “Nāgita, may I never become famous. May fame not come to me. | T: “Let me never come upon fame, Nāgita, and may fame never catch up with me.
an5.30:2.6 | S: There are those who can’t get the pleasure of renunciation, the pleasure of seclusion, the pleasure of peace, the pleasure of awakening when they want, without trouble or difficulty like I can. | T: One who does not gain at will, without trouble or difficulty, this bliss of renunciation, bliss of solitude, bliss of peace, bliss of enlightenment that I gain at will, without trouble or difficulty,
an5.30:2.7 | S: Let them enjoy the filthy, lazy pleasure of possessions, honor, and popularity.” | T: might accept that vile pleasure, that slothful pleasure, the pleasure of gain, honor, and praise.”
an5.30:3.1 | S: “Sir, may the Blessed One please relent now! May the Holy One relent! | T: “Let the Blessed One now consent, Bhante, let the Fortunate One consent.
an5.30:3.2 | S: Now is the time for the Buddha to relent. | T: This is now the time for the Blessed One to consent.
an5.30:3.3 | S: Wherever the Buddha now goes, the brahmins and householders, and people of town and country will incline the same way. | T: Wherever the Blessed One will go now, the brahmin householders of town and countryside will incline in the same direction.
an5.30:3.4 | S: It’s like when the heavens rain heavily and the water flows downhill. | T: Just as, when thick drops of rain are pouring down, the water flows down along the slope,
an5.30:3.5 | S: In the same way, wherever the Buddha now goes, the brahmins and householders, and people of town and country will incline the same way. | T: so too, wherever the Blessed One will go now, the brahmin householders of town and country will incline in the same direction.
an5.30:3.6 | S: Why is that? | T: For what reason?
an5.30:3.7 | S: Because of the Buddha’s ethics and wisdom.” | T: Because of the Blessed One’s virtuous behavior and wisdom.”
an5.30:4.1 | S: “Nāgita, may I never become famous. May fame not come to me. | T: “Let me never come upon fame, Nāgita, and may fame never catch up with me.
an5.30:4.2 | S: There are those who can’t get the pleasure of renunciation, the pleasure of seclusion, the pleasure of peace, the pleasure of awakening when they want, without trouble or difficulty like I can. | T: One who does not gain at will, without trouble or difficulty, this bliss of renunciation …
an5.30:4.3 | S: Let them enjoy the filthy, lazy pleasure of possessions, honor, and popularity. | T: might accept that vile pleasure, that slothful pleasure, the pleasure of gain, honor, and praise.
an5.30:4.4 | S: What you eat, drink, chew, and taste ends up as excrement and urine. | T: (1) “Nāgita, what is eaten, drunk, consumed, and tasted winds up as feces and urine:
an5.30:4.5 | S: This is its outcome. | T: this is its outcome.
an5.30:4.6 | S: When loved ones decay and perish, sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress arise. | T: (2) From the change and alteration of things that are dear arise sorrow, lamentation, pain, dejection, and anguish:
an5.30:4.7 | S: This is its outcome. | T: this is its outcome.
an5.30:4.8 | S: When you pursue meditation on the feature of ugliness, revulsion at the feature of beauty becomes stabilized. | T: (3) For one devoted to practicing meditation on the mark of unattractiveness, revulsion toward the mark of the beautiful becomes established:
an5.30:4.9 | S: This is its outcome. | T: this is its outcome.
an5.30:4.10 | S: When you meditate observing impermanence in the six fields of contact, revulsion at contact becomes stabilized. | T: (4) For one who dwells contemplating impermanence in the six bases for contact, revulsion toward contact becomes established:
an5.30:4.11 | S: This is its outcome. | T: this is its outcome.
an5.30:4.12 | S: When you meditate observing rise and fall in the five grasping aggregates, revulsion at grasping becomes stabilized. | T: (5) For one who dwells contemplating arising and vanishing in the five aggregates subject to clinging, revulsion toward clinging becomes established:
an5.30:4.13 | S: This is its outcome.” | T: this is its outcome.”
an5.30:4.14 | P: Dasamaṁ. | T: (none)
an5.30:4.15 | P: Pañcaṅgikavaggo tatiyo. | T: (none)
an5.30:5.0 | P: Tassuddānaṁ | T: (none)
an5.30:5.1 | P: Dve agāravupakkilesā, | T: (none)
an5.30:5.2 | P: Dussīlānuggahitena ca; | T: (none)
an5.30:5.3 | P: Vimuttisamādhipañcaṅgikā, | T: (none)
an5.30:5.4 | P: Caṅkamaṁ nāgitena cāti. | T: (none)

## an5.35
an5.35:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are five benefits of giving. | T: “Bhikkhus, there are these five benefits of giving.
an5.35:1.2 | S: What five? | T: What five?
an5.35:1.3 | S: A giver, a donor is dear and beloved by many people. | T: (1) One is dear and agreeable to many people.
an5.35:1.4 | S: Good and true persons associate with them. | T: (2) Good persons resort to one.
an5.35:1.5 | S: They get a good reputation. | T: (3) One acquires a good reputation.
an5.35:1.6 | S: They don’t neglect a layperson’s duties. | T: (4) One is not deficient in the layperson’s duties.
an5.35:1.7 | S: When their body breaks up, after death, they’re reborn in a good place, a heavenly realm. | T: (5) With the breakup of the body, after death, one is reborn in a good destination, in a heavenly world.
an5.35:1.8 | S: These are the five benefits of giving. | T: These are the five benefits in giving.”
an5.35:2.1 | S: Giving, one is loved, | T: By giving, one becomes dear,
an5.35:2.2 | S: and follows the way of the good. | T: one follows the duty of the good;
an5.35:2.3 | S: The good, disciplined spiritual practitioners | T: the good self-controlled monks
an5.35:2.4 | S: always associate with you. | T: always resort to one.
an5.35:3.1 | S: They teach you the Dhamma | T: They teach one the Dhamma
an5.35:3.2 | S: that casts aside all suffering. | T: that dispels all suffering,
an5.35:3.3 | S: Having understood this teaching, | T: having understood which
an5.35:3.4 | S: the undefiled one is fully extinguished.” | T: the taintless one here attains nibbāna.
an5.35:3.5 | P: Pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an5.36
an5.36:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are these five timely gifts. | T: “Bhikkhus, there are these five timely gifts.
an5.36:1.2 | S: What five? | T: What five?
an5.36:1.3 | S: A gift to a visitor. | T: (1) One gives a gift to a visitor.
an5.36:1.4 | S: A gift to someone setting out on a journey. | T: (2) One gives a gift to one setting out on a journey.
an5.36:1.5 | S: A gift to someone who is sick. | T: (3) One gives a gift to a patient.
an5.36:1.6 | S: A gift at a time of famine. | T: (4) One gives a gift during a famine.
an5.36:1.7 | S: Presenting the freshly harvested grains and fruits first to those who are ethical. | T: (5) One first presents the newly harvested crops and fruits to the virtuous ones.
an5.36:1.8 | S: These are the five timely gifts. | T: These are the five timely gifts.”
an5.36:2.1 | S: The wise give at the right time, | T: At the proper time, those wise,
an5.36:2.2 | S: being bountiful and rid of stinginess. | T: charitable, and generous folk
an5.36:2.3 | S: A religious donation at the right time | T: give a timely gift to the noble ones,
an5.36:2.4 | S: to the noble ones, upright and unaffected, | T: who are stable and upright;
an5.36:3.1 | S: given with a clear and confident mind, | T: given with a clear mind,
an5.36:3.2 | S: is indeed abundant. | T: one’s offering is vast.
an5.36:3.3 | S: Those who rejoice at that, | T: Those who rejoice in such deeds
an5.36:3.4 | S: or do other services, | T: or who provide other service
an5.36:3.5 | S: don’t miss out on the offering; | T: do not miss out on the offering;
an5.36:3.6 | S: they too have a share in the merit. | T: they too partake of the merit.
an5.36:4.1 | S: So you should give without holding back, | T: Therefore, with a non-regressing mind,
an5.36:4.2 | S: where a gift is very fruitful. | T: one should give a gift where it yields great fruit.
an5.36:4.3 | S: The good deeds of sentient beings | T: Merits are the support of living beings
an5.36:4.4 | S: support them in the next world.” | T: when they arise in the other world.
an5.36:4.5 | P: Chaṭṭhaṁ. | T: (none)

## an5.37
an5.37:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, when a giver gives food, they give the recipients five things. | T: “Bhikkhus, a donor who gives food gives the recipients five things.
an5.37:1.2 | S: What five? | T: What five?
an5.37:1.3 | S: Long life, beauty, happiness, strength, and eloquence. | T: One gives life, beauty, happiness, strength, and discernment.
an5.37:1.4 | S: Giving long life, they have long life as a god or human. | T: (1) Having given life, one partakes of life, whether celestial or human.
an5.37:1.5 | S: Giving beauty, they have beauty as a god or human. | T: (2) Having given beauty, one partakes of beauty, whether celestial or human.
an5.37:1.6 | S: Giving happiness, they have happiness as a god or human. | T: (3) Having given happiness, one partakes of happiness, whether celestial or human.
an5.37:1.7 | S: Giving strength, they have strength as a god or human. | T: (4) Having given strength, one partakes of strength, whether celestial or human.
an5.37:1.8 | S: Giving eloquence, they are eloquent as a god or human. | T: (5) Having given discernment, one partakes of discernment, whether celestial or human.
an5.37:1.9 | S: When a giver gives food, they give the recipients five things. | T: A donor who gives food gives the recipients these five things.”
an5.37:2.1 | S: Being attentive, one is a giver of life, strength, | T: The wise one is a giver of life, strength,
an5.37:2.2 | S: beauty, and eloquence. | T: beauty, and discernment.
an5.37:2.3 | S: An intelligent giver of happiness | T: The intelligent one is a donor of happiness
an5.37:2.4 | S: gains happiness in return. | T: and in turn acquires happiness.
an5.37:3.1 | S: Giving life, strength, beauty, | T: Having given life, strength, beauty,
an5.37:3.2 | S: happiness, and eloquence, | T: happiness, and discernment,
an5.37:3.3 | S: they’re long-lived and famous | T: one is long-lived and famous
an5.37:3.4 | S: wherever they’re reborn.” | T: wherever one is reborn.
an5.37:3.5 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an5.39
an5.39:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, parents see five reasons to wish for the birth of a child in the family. | T: “Bhikkhus, considering five prospects, mother and father wish for a son to be born in their family.
an5.39:1.2 | S: What five? | T: What five?
an5.39:1.3 | S: Since we looked after them, they’ll look after us. | T: (1) ‘Having been supported by us, he will support us.
an5.39:1.4 | S: They’ll do their duty for us. | T: (2) Or he will do work for us.
an5.39:1.5 | S: The family customs will last. | T: (3) Our family lineage will be extended.
an5.39:1.6 | S: They’ll take care of the inheritance. | T: (4) He will manage the inheritance,
an5.39:1.7 | S: Or else when we have passed away they’ll give an offering on our behalf. | T: (5) or else, when we have passed on, he will give an offering on our behalf.’
an5.39:1.8 | S: Parents see these five reasons to wish for the birth of a child in the family. | T: Considering these five prospects, mother and father wish for a son to be born in their family.”
an5.39:2.1 | S: Seeing five reasons, | T: Considering the five prospects,
an5.39:2.2 | S: astute people wish for a child. | T: wise people wish for a son.
an5.39:2.3 | S: Since we looked after them, they’ll look after us. | T: “Supported by us, he will support us,
an5.39:2.4 | S: They’ll do their duty for us. | T: or he will do work for us.
an5.39:3.1 | S: The family customs will last. | T: The family lineage will be extended,
an5.39:3.2 | S: They’ll take care of the inheritance. | T: he will manage the inheritance,
an5.39:3.3 | S: Or else when we have passed away | T: or else, when we have passed on,
an5.39:3.4 | S: they’ll give an offering on our behalf. | T: he will make an offering on our behalf.”
an5.39:4.1 | S: Seeing these five reasons | T: Considering these prospects,
an5.39:4.2 | S: astute people wish for a child. | T: wise people wish for a son.
an5.39:4.3 | S: And so true persons, | T: Therefore good persons,
an5.39:4.4 | S: grateful and thankful, | T: grateful and appreciative,
an5.39:5.1 | S: look after their parents, | T: support their mother and father,
an5.39:5.2 | S: remembering past deeds. | T: recalling how they helped one in the past;
an5.39:5.3 | S: They do for their parents, | T: they do what is necessary for them
an5.39:5.4 | S: as their parents did for them in the past. | T: as they did for oneself in the past.
an5.39:6.1 | S: Following their advice, <j>looking after those who raised them, | T: Following their advice, nurturing those who brought him up,
an5.39:6.2 | S: the family customs are not lost. | T: continuing the family lineage,
an5.39:6.3 | S: Faithful, accomplished in ethics, | T: endowed with faith, virtuous:
an5.39:6.4 | S: such a child is praiseworthy.” | T: this son is worthy of praise.
an5.39:6.5 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an5.3
an5.3:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, when a mendicant has five qualities they live unhappily in this very life—with distress, anguish, and fever—and when the body breaks up, after death, they can expect a bad rebirth. | T: “Bhikkhus, possessing five qualities, a bhikkhu dwells in suffering in this very life—with distress, anguish, and fever—and with the breakup of the body, after death, a bad destination can be expected for him.
an5.3:1.2 | S: What five? | T: What five?
an5.3:1.3 | S: It’s when a mendicant is faithless, shameless, imprudent, lazy, and witless. | T: Here, a bhikkhu is devoid of faith, morally shameless, morally reckless, lazy, and unwise.
an5.3:1.4 | S: When a mendicant possesses these five qualities they live unhappily in this very life—with distress, anguish, and fever—and when the body breaks up, after death, they can expect a bad rebirth. | T: Possessing these five qualities, a bhikkhu dwells in suffering in this very life—with distress, anguish, and fever—and with the breakup of the body, after death, he can expect a bad destination.
an5.3:2.1 | S: When a mendicant has five qualities they live happily in this very life—without distress, anguish, or fever—and when the body breaks up, after death, they can expect a good rebirth. | T: “Bhikkhus, possessing five other qualities, a bhikkhu dwells happily in this very life—without distress, anguish, and fever—and with the breakup of the body, after death, a good destination can be expected for him.
an5.3:2.2 | S: What five? | T: What five?
an5.3:2.3 | S: It’s when a mendicant is faithful, conscientious, prudent, energetic, and wise. | T: Here, a bhikkhu is endowed with faith, has a sense of moral shame, has moral dread, and is energetic and wise.
an5.3:2.4 | S: When a mendicant possesses these five qualities they live happily in this very life—without distress, anguish, or fever—and when the body breaks up, after death, they can expect a good rebirth.” | T: Possessing these five qualities, a bhikkhu dwells happily in this very life—without distress, anguish, and fever—and with the breakup of the body, after death, a good destination can be expected for him.”
an5.3:2.5 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an5.41
an5.41:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling at Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s Park.
an5.41:1.2 | S: Then the householder Anāthapiṇḍika went up to the Buddha, bowed, and sat down to one side. The Buddha said to him: | T: Then the householder Anāthapiṇḍika approached the Blessed One, paid homage to him, and sat down to one side. The Blessed One then said to him:
an5.41:1.3 | S: “Householder, there are these five reasons to get rich. | T: “Householder, there are these five utilizations of wealth.
an5.41:1.4 | S: What five? | T: What five?
an5.41:1.5 | S: Firstly, with his legitimate wealth—earned by his efforts and initiative, built up with his own hands, gathered by the sweat of the brow—a noble disciple makes himself happy and pleased, keeping himself properly happy. | T: (1) “Here, householder, with wealth acquired by energetic striving, amassed by the strength of his arms, earned by the sweat of his brow, righteous wealth righteously gained, the noble disciple makes himself happy and pleased and properly maintains himself in happiness;
an5.41:1.6 | S: He makes his mother and father happy … | T: he makes his parents happy and pleased and properly maintains them in happiness;

