/bin/bash -lc "sed -n '201,400p' /home/kjn/CodeBase/gabriel/codex-handoff/bodhi/batch-061.txt" in /home/kjn/CodeBase/gabriel/codex-handoff
 succeeded in 0ms:
an5.41:1.7 | S: He makes his children, partners, bondservants, workers, and staff happy … | T: he makes his wife and children, his slaves, workers, and servants happy and pleased and properly maintains them in happiness.
an5.41:1.8 | S: This is the first reason to get rich. | T: This is the first utilization of wealth.
an5.41:2.1 | S: Furthermore, with his legitimate wealth he makes his friends and colleagues happy … | T: (2) “Again, with wealth acquired by energetic striving … righteously gained, the noble disciple makes his friends and companions happy and pleased and properly maintains them in happiness.
an5.41:2.2 | S: This is the second reason to get rich. | T: This is the second utilization of wealth.
an5.41:3.1 | S: Furthermore, with his legitimate wealth he protects himself against losses from such things as fire, water, kings, bandits, or unloved heirs. | T: (3) “Again, with wealth acquired by energetic striving … righteously gained, the noble disciple makes provisions with his wealth against the losses that might arise because of
an5.41:3.2 | P: aggito vā udakato vā rājato vā corato vā appiyato vā dāyādato— | T: fire or floods, kings or bandits or unloved heirs;
an5.41:3.3 | S: He keeps himself safe. | T: he makes himself secure against them.
an5.41:3.4 | S: This is the third reason to get rich. | T: This is the third utilization of wealth.
an5.41:4.1 | S: Furthermore, with his legitimate wealth he makes five spirit-offerings: | T: (4) “Again, with wealth acquired by energetic striving … righteously gained, the noble disciple makes the five oblations:
an5.41:4.2 | S: to relatives, guests, ancestors, king, and deities. | T: to relatives, guests, ancestors, the king, and the deities.
an5.41:4.3 | S: This is the fourth reason to get rich. | T: This is the fourth utilization of wealth.
an5.41:5.1 | S: Furthermore, with his legitimate wealth he establishes an uplifting religious donation for ascetics and brahmins—those who refrain from intoxication and negligence, are settled in patience and gentleness, and who tame, calm, and extinguish themselves—that’s conducive to heaven, ripens in happiness, and leads to heaven. | T: (5) “Again, with wealth acquired by energetic striving … … righteously gained, the noble disciple establishes an uplifting offering of alms—an offering that is heavenly, resulting in happiness, conducive to heaven—to those ascetics and brahmins who refrain from intoxication and heedlessness, who are settled in patience and mildness, who tame themselves, calm themselves, and train themselves for nibbāna.
an5.41:5.2 | S: This is the fifth reason to get rich. | T: This is the fifth utilization of wealth.
an5.41:5.3 | S: These are the five reasons to get rich. | T: “These, householder, are the five utilizations of wealth.
an5.41:6.1 | S: Now if the riches a noble disciple gets for these five reasons run out, he thinks: | T: Householder, if a noble disciple’s wealth is exhausted when he has utilized it in these five ways, he thinks:
an5.41:6.2 | S: ‘So, the riches I have obtained for these reasons are running out.’ | T: ‘I have utilized wealth in these five ways and my wealth is exhausted.’
an5.41:6.3 | S: And so he has no regrets. | T: Thus he has no regret.
an5.41:6.4 | S: But if the riches a noble disciple gets for these five reasons increase, he thinks: | T: But if a noble disciple’s wealth increases when he has utilized it in these five ways, he thinks:
an5.41:6.5 | S: ‘So, the riches I have obtained for these reasons are increasing.’ | T: ‘I have utilized wealth in these five ways and my wealth has increased.’
an5.41:6.6 | S: And so he has no regrets in both cases. | T: Thus, either way, he has no regret.”
an5.41:7.1 | S: ‘I’ve enjoyed my wealth, <j>supporting those who depend on me; | T: “I’ve enjoyed wealth, supported my dependents,
an5.41:7.2 | S: I’ve overcome losses; | T: and overcome adversities.
an5.41:7.3 | S: I’ve given uplifting religious donations; | T: I have given an uplifting offering,
an5.41:7.4 | S: and made the five spirit-offerings. | T: and performed the five oblations.
an5.41:7.5 | S: I have looked after the ethical and | T: I have served the virtuous monks,
an5.41:7.6 | S: disciplined spiritual practitioners. | T: the self-controlled celibate ones.
an5.41:8.1 | S: I’ve achieved the goal | T: “I have achieved whatever purpose
an5.41:8.2 | S: for which an astute layperson | T: a wise person, dwelling at home,
an5.41:8.3 | S: wishes to gain wealth. | T: might have in desiring wealth;
an5.41:8.4 | S: I don’t regret what I’ve done.’ | T: what I have done brings me no regret.”
an5.41:9.1 | S: A mortal man who recollects this | T: Recollecting this, a mortal
an5.41:9.2 | S: stands firm in the teaching of the noble ones. | T: remains firm in the noble Dhamma.
an5.41:9.3 | S: They’re praised in this life by the astute, | T: They praise him here in this life,
an5.41:9.4 | S: and they depart to rejoice in heaven.” | T: and after death he rejoices in heaven.
an5.41:9.5 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an5.46
an5.46:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are five accomplishments. | T: “Bhikkhus, there are these five accomplishments.
an5.46:1.2 | S: What five? | T: What five?
an5.46:1.3 | S: Accomplishment in faith, ethics, learning, generosity, and wisdom. | T: Accomplishment in faith, accomplishment in virtuous behavior, accomplishment in learning, accomplishment in generosity, and accomplishment in wisdom.
an5.46:1.4 | S: These are the five accomplishments.” | T: These are the five accomplishments.”
an5.46:1.5 | P: Chaṭṭhaṁ. | T: (none)

## an5.4
an5.4:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, a mendicant with five qualities is placed in hell as if delivered there. | T: “Bhikkhus, possessing five qualities, a bhikkhu is deposited in hell as if brought there.
an5.4:1.2 | S: What five? | T: What five?
an5.4:1.3 | S: It’s when a mendicant is faithless, shameless, imprudent, lazy, and witless. | T: Here, a bhikkhu is devoid of faith, morally shameless, morally reckless, lazy, and unwise.
an5.4:1.4 | S: A mendicant with these five qualities is placed in hell as if delivered there. | T: Possessing these five qualities, a bhikkhu is deposited in hell as if brought there.
an5.4:2.1 | S: A mendicant with five qualities is placed in heaven as if delivered there. | T: “Bhikkhus, possessing five other qualities, a bhikkhu is deposited in heaven as if brought there.
an5.4:2.2 | S: What five? | T: What five?
an5.4:2.3 | S: It’s when a mendicant is faithful, conscientious, prudent, energetic, and wise. | T: Here, a bhikkhu is endowed with faith, has a sense of moral shame, has moral dread, and is energetic and wise.
an5.4:2.4 | S: A mendicant with these five qualities is placed in heaven as if delivered there.” | T: Possessing these five qualities, a bhikkhu is deposited in heaven as if brought there.”
an5.4:2.5 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## an5.51
an5.51:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: Thus have I heard.
an5.51:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling at Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s Park.
an5.51:1.3 | S: There the Buddha addressed the mendicants, | T: There the Blessed One addressed the bhikkhus:
an5.51:1.4 | S: “Mendicants!” | T: “Bhikkhus!”
an5.51:1.5 | S: “Venerable sir,” they replied. | T: “Venerable sir!” those bhikkhus replied.
an5.51:1.6 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said this:
an5.51:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are these five obstacles and hindrances, parasites of the mind that weaken wisdom. | T: “Bhikkhus, there are these five obstructions, hindrances, encumbrances of the mind, states that weaken wisdom.
an5.51:2.2 | S: What five? | T: What five?
an5.51:2.3 | S: Sensual desire … | T: (1) Sensual desire is an obstruction, a hindrance, an encumbrance of the mind, a state that weakens wisdom.
an5.51:2.4 | S: Ill will … | T: (2) Ill will …
an5.51:2.5 | S: Dullness and drowsiness … | T: (3) Dullness and drowsiness …
an5.51:2.6 | S: Restlessness and remorse … | T: (4) Restlessness and remorse …
an5.51:2.7 | S: Doubt … | T: (5) Doubt is an obstruction, a hindrance, an encumbrance of the mind, a state that weakens wisdom.
an5.51:2.8 | S: These are the five obstacles and hindrances, parasites of the mind that weaken wisdom. | T: These are the five obstructions, hindrances, encumbrances of the mind, states that weaken wisdom.
an5.51:3.1 | S: Take a mendicant who has feeble and weak wisdom, not having given up these five obstacles and hindrances, parasites of the mind that weaken wisdom. It is quite impossible that they would know what’s for their own good, the good of another, or the good of both; or that they would realize any superhuman distinction in knowledge and vision worthy of the noble ones. | T: “Bhikkhus, without having abandoned these five obstructions, hindrances, encumbrances of the mind, states that weaken wisdom, it is impossible that a bhikkhu, with his powerless and feeble wisdom, might know his own good, the good of others, or the good of both, or realize a superhuman distinction in knowledge and vision worthy of the noble ones.
an5.51:3.2 | S: Suppose there was a mountain river that flowed swiftly, going far, carrying all before it. | T: Suppose a river were flowing down from a mountain, traveling a long distance, with a swift current, carrying along much flotsam.
an5.51:3.3 | S: But then a man would open channels on both sides, | T: Then, on both of its banks, a man would open irrigation channels.
an5.51:3.4 | S: so the mid-river current would be dispersed, displaced, and separated. The river would no longer flow swiftly, going far, carrying all before it. | T: In such a case, the current in the middle of the river would be dispersed, spread out, and divided, so that the river would no longer travel a long distance, with a swift current, carrying along much flotsam.
an5.51:3.5 | S: In the same way, take a mendicant who has feeble and weak wisdom, not having given up these five obstacles and hindrances, parasites of the mind that weaken wisdom. It is not possible that they would know what’s for their own good, the good of another, or the good of both; or that they would realize any superhuman distinction in knowledge and vision worthy of the noble ones. | T: So too, without having abandoned these five obstructions … it is impossible that a bhikkhu … might realize a superhuman distinction in knowledge and vision worthy of the noble ones.
an5.51:4.1 | S: Take a mendicant who has powerful wisdom, having given up these five obstacles and hindrances, parasites of the mind that weaken wisdom. It’s quite possible that they would know what’s for their own good, the good of another, or the good of both; or that they would realize any superhuman distinction in knowledge and vision worthy of the noble ones. | T: “But, bhikkhus, having abandoned these five obstructions, hindrances, encumbrances of the mind, states that weaken wisdom, it is possible that a bhikkhu, with his powerful wisdom, might know his own good, the good of others, and the good of both, and realize a superhuman distinction in knowledge and vision worthy of the noble ones.
an5.51:4.2 | S: Suppose there was a mountain river that flowed swiftly, going far, carrying all before it. | T: Suppose a river were flowing down from a mountain, traveling a long distance, with a swift current, carrying along much flotsam.
an5.51:4.3 | S: But then a man would close up the channels on both sides, | T: Then a man would close up the irrigation channels on both of its banks.
an5.51:4.4 | S: so the mid-river current would not be dispersed, displaced, and separated. The river would keep flowing swiftly for a long way, carrying all before it. | T: In such a case, the current in the middle of the river would not be dispersed, spread out, and divided, so that the river could travel a long distance, with a swift current, carrying along much flotsam.
an5.51:4.5 | S: In the same way, take a mendicant who has powerful wisdom, having given up these five obstacles and hindrances, parasites of the mind that weaken wisdom. It’s quite possible that they would know what’s for their own good, the good of another, or the good of both; or that they would realize any superhuman distinction in knowledge and vision worthy of the noble ones.” | T: So too, having abandoned these five obstructions … it is possible that a bhikkhu … might realize a superhuman distinction in knowledge and vision worthy of the noble ones.”
an5.51:4.6 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an5.52
an5.52:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, rightly speaking, you’d call the five hindrances a ‘heap of the unskillful’. | T: “Bhikkhus, saying ‘a heap of the unwholesome,’ it is about the five hindrances that one could rightly say this.
an5.52:1.2 | S: For these five hindrances are entirely a heap of the unskillful. | T: For the five hindrances are a complete heap of the unwholesome.
an5.52:1.3 | S: What five? | T: What five?
an5.52:1.4 | S: The hindrances of sensual desire, ill will, dullness and drowsiness, restlessness and remorse, and doubt. | T: The hindrance of sensual desire, the hindrance of ill will, the hindrance of dullness and drowsiness, the hindrance of restlessness and remorse, and the hindrance of doubt.
an5.52:1.5 | S: Rightly speaking, you’d call these five hindrances a ‘heap of the unskillful’. | T: Bhikkhus, saying ‘a heap of the unwholesome,’ it is about these five hindrances that one could rightly say this.
an5.52:1.6 | S: For these five hindrances are entirely a heap of the unskillful.” | T: For these five hindrances are a complete heap of the unwholesome.”
an5.52:1.7 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an5.53
an5.53:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are these five factors that support meditation. | T: “Bhikkhus, there are these five factors that assist striving.
an5.53:1.2 | S: What five? | T: What five?
an5.53:1.3 | S: It’s when a mendicant has faith in the Realized One’s awakening: | T: (1) “Here, a bhikkhu is endowed with faith. He places faith in the enlightenment of the Tathāgata thus:
an5.53:1.4 | S: ‘That Blessed One is perfected, a fully awakened Buddha, accomplished in knowledge and conduct, holy, knower of the world, supreme guide for those fit for training, teacher of gods and humans, awakened, blessed.’ | T: ‘The Blessed One is an arahant, perfectly enlightened, accomplished in true knowledge and conduct, fortunate, knower of the world, unsurpassed trainer of persons to be tamed, teacher of devas and humans, the Enlightened One, the Blessed One.’
an5.53:1.5 | S: They are rarely ill or unwell. | T: (2) “He is seldom ill or afflicted,
an5.53:1.6 | S: Their stomach digests well, being neither too hot nor too cold, but just right, and fit for meditation. | T: possessing an even digestion that is neither too cool nor too hot but moderate and suitable for striving.
an5.53:1.7 | S: They’re not devious or deceitful. | T: (3) “He is honest and open,
an5.53:1.8 | S: They reveal themselves honestly to the Teacher or sensible spiritual companions. | T: one who reveals himself as he really is to the Teacher and his wise fellow monks.
an5.53:1.9 | S: They live with energy roused up for giving up unskillful qualities and embracing skillful qualities. They’re strong, staunchly vigorous, not slacking off when it comes to developing skillful qualities. | T: (4) “He has aroused energy for abandoning unwholesome qualities and acquiring wholesome qualities; he is strong, firm in exertion, not casting off the duty of cultivating wholesome qualities.
an5.53:1.10 | S: They’re wise. They have the wisdom of arising and passing away which is noble, penetrative, and leads to the complete ending of suffering. | T: (5) “He is wise; he possesses the wisdom that discerns arising and passing away, which is noble and penetrative and leads to the complete destruction of suffering.
an5.53:1.11 | S: These are the five factors that support meditation.” | T: “These, bhikkhus, are the five factors that assist striving.”
an5.53:1.12 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an5.54
an5.54:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are five times that are not good for meditation. | T: “Bhikkhus, there are these five unfavorable occasions for striving.
an5.54:1.2 | S: What five? | T: What five?
an5.54:1.3 | S: Firstly, a mendicant is old, overcome with old age. | T: (1) “Here, a bhikkhu is old, overcome by old age.
an5.54:1.4 | S: This is the first time that’s not good for meditation. | T: This is the first unfavorable occasion for striving.
an5.54:2.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant is sick, overcome by sickness. | T: (2) “Again, a bhikkhu is ill, overcome by illness.
an5.54:2.2 | S: This is the second time that’s not good for meditation. | T: This is the second unfavorable occasion for striving.
an5.54:3.1 | S: Furthermore, there’s a famine, a bad harvest, so it’s hard to get almsfood, and not easy to keep going by collecting alms. | T: (3) “Again, there is a famine, a poor harvest, a time when almsfood is difficult to obtain and it is not easy to subsist by means of gleaning.
an5.54:3.2 | S: This is the third time that’s not good for meditation. | T: This is the third unfavorable occasion for striving.
an5.54:4.1 | S: Furthermore, there’s peril due to turmoil in the wilds, so the countryfolk mount their vehicles and flee everywhere. | T: (4) “Again, there is peril, turbulence in the wilderness, and the people of the countryside, mounted on their vehicles, flee on all sides.
an5.54:4.2 | S: This is the fourth time that’s not good for meditation. | T: This is the fourth unfavorable occasion for striving.
an5.54:5.1 | S: Furthermore, there’s a schism in the Saṅgha. | T: (5) “Again, there is a schism in the Saṅgha,
an5.54:5.2 | S: When the Saṅgha is split, they abuse, insult, block, and forsake each other. | T: and when there is a schism in the Saṅgha there are mutual insults, mutual reviling, mutual disparagement, and mutual rejection.
an5.54:5.3 | S: This doesn’t inspire confidence in those without it, and it causes some with confidence to change their minds. | T: Then those without confidence do not gain confidence, while some of those with confidence change their minds.
an5.54:5.4 | S: This is the fifth time that’s not good for meditation. | T: This is the fifth unfavorable occasion for striving.
an5.54:5.5 | S: These are the five times that are not good for meditation. | T: “These are the five unfavorable occasions for striving.
an5.54:6.1 | S: There are five times that are good for meditation. | T: “There are, bhikkhus, these five favorable occasions for striving.
an5.54:6.2 | S: What five? | T: What five?
an5.54:6.3 | S: Firstly, a mendicant is a youth, young, with pristine black hair, blessed with youth, in the prime of life. | T: (1) “Here, a bhikkhu is young, a black-haired young man endowed with the blessing of youth, in the prime of life.
an5.54:6.4 | S: This is the first time that’s good for meditation. | T: This is the first favorable occasion for striving.
an5.54:7.1 | S: Furthermore, they are rarely ill or unwell. Their stomach digests well, being neither too hot nor too cold, but just right, and fit for meditation. | T: (2) “Again, a bhikkhu is seldom ill or afflicted, possessing an even digestion that is neither too cool nor too hot but moderate and suitable for striving.
an5.54:7.2 | S: This is the second time that’s good for meditation. | T: This is the second favorable occasion for striving.
an5.54:8.1 | S: Furthermore, there’s plenty of food, a good harvest, so it’s easy to get almsfood, and easy to keep going by collecting alms. | T: (3) “Again, food is plentiful; there has been a good harvest and almsfood is abundant, so that one can easily sustain oneself by means of gleaning.
an5.54:8.2 | S: This is the third time that’s good for meditation. | T: This is the third favorable occasion for striving.
an5.54:9.1 | S: Furthermore, people live in harmony, appreciating each other, without dispute, blending like milk and water, and regarding each other with kindly eyes. | T: (4) “Again, people are dwelling in concord, harmoniously, without disputes, blending like milk and water, viewing each other with eyes of affection.
an5.54:9.2 | S: This is the fourth time that’s good for meditation. | T: This is the fourth favorable occasion for striving.
an5.54:10.1 | S: Furthermore, the Saṅgha lives comfortably, in harmony, appreciating each other, without dispute, with one recitation. | T: (5) “Again, the Saṅgha is dwelling at ease—in concord, harmoniously, without disputes, with a single recitation.
an5.54:10.2 | S: When the Saṅgha is in harmony, they don’t abuse, insult, block, or forsake each other. | T: When the Saṅgha is in concord, there are no mutual insults, no mutual reviling, no mutual disparagement, and no mutual rejection.
an5.54:10.3 | S: This inspires confidence in those without it, and increases confidence in those who have it. | T: Then those without confidence gain confidence and those with confidence increase in their confidence.
an5.54:10.4 | S: This is the fifth time that’s good for meditation. | T: This is the fifth favorable occasion for striving.
an5.54:10.5 | S: These are the five times that are good for meditation.” | T: “These are the five favorable occasions for striving.”
an5.54:10.6 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## an5.59
an5.59:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, it’s hard to find someone gone forth when old who has five qualities. | T: “Bhikkhus, it is rare to find one gone forth in old age who possesses five qualities.
an5.59:1.2 | S: What five? | T: What five?
an5.59:1.3 | S: It’s hard to find someone gone forth when old who is sophisticated, well-mannered, and learned, who can teach Dhamma, and has memorized the monastic law. | T: It is rare to find one gone forth in old age (1) who is astute; (2) who has the proper manner; (3) who is learned; (4) who can speak on the Dhamma; and (5) who is an expert on the discipline.
an5.59:1.4 | S: It’s hard to find someone gone forth when old who possesses these five qualities.” | T: It is rare to find one gone forth in old age who possesses these five qualities.”
an5.59:1.5 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an5.5
an5.5:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, any monk or nun who resigns the training and returns to a lesser life deserves rebuttal and criticism on five legitimate grounds in this very life. | T: “Bhikkhus, any bhikkhu or bhikkhunī who gives up the training and reverts to the lower life incurs five reasonable criticisms and grounds for censure in this very life.
an5.5:1.2 | S: What five? | T: What five?
an5.5:1.3 | S: ‘You had no faith, conscience, prudence, energy, or wisdom regarding skillful qualities.’ | T: (1) ‘You did not have faith in cultivating wholesome qualities. (2) You did not have a sense of moral shame in cultivating wholesome qualities. (3) You did not have moral dread in cultivating wholesome qualities. (4) You did not have energy in cultivating wholesome qualities. (5) You did not have wisdom in cultivating wholesome qualities.’
an5.5:1.4 | S: Any monk or nun who resigns the training and returns to a lesser life deserves rebuttal and criticism on these five legitimate grounds in this very life. | T: Any bhikkhu or bhikkhunī who gives up the training and reverts to the lower life incurs these five reasonable criticisms and grounds for censure in this very life.
an5.5:2.1 | S: Any monk or nun who lives the full and pure spiritual life in pain and sadness, weeping, with tearful face, deserves praise on five legitimate grounds in this very life. | T: “Bhikkhus, any bhikkhu or bhikkhunī who lives the complete and pure spiritual life, even with pain and dejection, weeping with a tearful face, gains five reasonable grounds for praise in this very life.
an5.5:2.2 | S: What five? | T: What five?
an5.5:2.3 | S: ‘You had faith, conscience, prudence, energy, and wisdom regarding skillful qualities.’ | T: (1) ‘You have had faith in cultivating wholesome qualities. (2) You have had a sense of moral shame in cultivating wholesome qualities. (3) You have had moral dread in cultivating wholesome qualities. (4) You have had energy in cultivating wholesome qualities. (5) You have had wisdom in cultivating wholesome qualities.’
an5.5:2.4 | S: Any monk or nun who lives the full and pure spiritual life in pain and sadness, weeping, with tearful face, deserves praise on these five legitimate grounds in this very life.” | T: Any bhikkhu or bhikkhunī who lives the complete and pure spiritual life, even with pain and dejection, weeping with a tearful face, gains these five reasonable grounds for praise in this very life.”
an5.5:2.5 | P: Pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an5.60
an5.60:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, it’s hard to find someone gone forth when old who has five qualities. | T: “Bhikkhus, it is rare to find one gone forth in old age who possesses five qualities.
an5.60:1.2 | S: What five? | T: What five?
an5.60:1.3 | S: It’s hard to find someone gone forth when old who is easy to admonish, retains what they learn, and learns respectfully, who can teach the Dhamma, and has memorized the monastic law. | T: It is rare to find one gone forth in old age (1) who is easy to correct; (2) who firmly retains in mind what he has learned; (3) who accepts instruction respectfully; (4) who can speak on the Dhamma; and (5) who is an expert on the discipline.
an5.60:1.4 | S: It’s hard to find someone gone forth when old who possesses these five qualities.” | T: It is rare to find one gone forth in old age who possesses these five qualities.”
an5.60:1.5 | P: Dasamaṁ. | T: (none)
an5.60:1.6 | P: Nīvaraṇavaggo paṭhamo. | T: (none)
an5.60:2.0 | P: Tassuddānaṁ | T: (none)
an5.60:2.1 | P: Āvaraṇaṁ rāsi aṅgāni, | T: (none)
an5.60:2.2 | P: samayaṁ mātuputtikā; | T: (none)
an5.60:2.3 | P: Upajjhā ṭhānā licchavi, | T: (none)
an5.60:2.4 | P: kumārā aparā duveti. | T: (none)


